Ladybird now starts a middle-button autoscroll over a nested document
that can’t scroll on the box that the nested document’s container
element is in, and tracks the pointer in screen coordinates at the page
level, so moves, presses, and releases over any document drive and end
it. Blink’s LayoutBox::FindAutoscrollable() walks up the layout tree to
the first user-scrollable box, continuing from a document’s LocalOwner()
in the parent frame. Its AutoscrollController belongs to the Page: Every
frame’s EventHandler hands it the pointer’s screen position through
HandleMouseMoveForMiddleClickAutoscroll(), and a press in any frame
stops it (EventHandler::HandleMousePressEvent()).

Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 don’t start middle-button
autoscroll in headless macOS runs, so the comparison comes from Blink’s
source. Firefox’s bug 2075178 reports the same culmus.sourceforge.io
symptom in Firefox, with a video of Chrome scrolling as expected.
